WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.409
The world outside of central Maine almost
never got to know Stephen King.

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:07.950
If not for his wife’s diligence and her
confidence in her husband, the book that launched

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.019
a million pages might never have come into
being.

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:14.799
When his wife Tabitha rescued the start of
the manuscript of “Carrie” from the trash

00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:19.250
and insisted her husband finish it, King was
working as an English teacher and writing

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.280
on the side.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.830
Tabitha’s judgment was right, Carrie became
a smash hit, and Stephen King is one of the

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.300
world’s most famous and most prolific authors.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.009
So what’s the story behind the stories?

00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:32.561
Let’s delve into his life...

00:00:32.561 --> 00:00:48.930
Early Life

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.050
Stephen King was born in Portland, Maine,
the largest city of the mostly rural state

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.340
that serves as the setting for so many of
his famous stories.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.960
Stephen was the second son born to Nellie
and Donald King, but the family of four soon

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.790
became a family of three.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:07.320
When Stephen was only two years old, and his
brother only four, their father went out,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.300
telling the family he was buying a pack of
cigarettes.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.670
He never returned.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.860
King’s mother worked several jobs, moving
with the boys from Maine, going from state

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.650
to state to find work and a place she could
afford to live and raise two boys on her own.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.100
But Maine was home, and it’s where the family
eventually settled for good.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.590
When they moved back to Maine, the family
didn’t have indoor plumbing.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.050
And this was the 1960s.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:32.320
King’s life growing up was a far cry from
the wealth he has now.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.850
But King didn’t see his childhood as exceptional
or out of the ordinary.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.120
Though he does acknowledge he’s always liked
scary things.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.920
“My childhood was pretty ordinary, except
from a very early age I wanted to be scared.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:44.990
I just did.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:46.140
I was scared afterward.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.960
I wanted a light on because I was scared.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.630
There was something in the closet.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.710
My imagination was very active even at a young
age.”

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:57.810
Some have said that King might also be inspired
to write such horrifying stories by a childhood

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.460
event he doesn’t even remember.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.190
"According to Mom, I had gone off to play
at a neighbor’s house—a house that was

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:04.350
near a railroad line.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.220
About an hour after I left I came back (she
said), as white as a ghost.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.010
I would not speak for the rest of the day;
I would not tell her why I’d not waited

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.370
to be picked up or phoned that I wanted to
come home; I would not tell her why my chum’s

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.670
mom hadn’t walked me back but had allowed
me to come alone.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.950
It turned out that the kid I had been playing
with had been run over by a freight train

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:27.150
while playing on or crossing the tracks (years
later, my mother told me they had picked up

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.340
the pieces in a wicker basket).”

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.230
As traumatic as that event must have been,
it was not the only explanation for King’s

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.400
vivid imagination.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.540
Friends of the King tell stories about how
the family was known for their attention to

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:39.540
literature.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.480
If their mother couldn’t find - or couldn’t
afford - a babysitter, she’d leave her sons

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.569
alone with the expectation they would read
aloud to each other.

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:49.660
King’s love of stories and the written word
was fostered from an early age.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:54.280
And, the tradition stuck - King and his wife
Tabitha also made their own kids read aloud

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.190
to each other, and to them.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.450
He’d even record them on cassettes to make
the family their own collection of audiobooks.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.520
Growing up, King also wrote material for his
brother’s newsletter.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.290
Called “Dave’s Rag,” they made copies
on a mimeograph machine and distributed it

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.290
to their friends.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.660
But King was soon able to move beyond just
writing for his sibling’s newsletter.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:15.989
In 1965, when he was still in high school,
King was published in Comics Review.

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:20.000
The story was right in line with the frightening
plots we all know King for today.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.459
King had been working as a gravedigger to
earn money as a teenager.

00:03:23.459 --> 00:03:27.790
The job inspired him to write a story called
“I Was A Teenage Grave Robber,” and its

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:31.160
publication was his first taste of published
success.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.180
The only downside - he didn’t get paid for
the work.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.740
His first paid published work didn’t come
until he was in college and earned $35 for

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.099
a story called “The Glass Floor.”

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:44.990
King graduated from Lisbon Falls High School,
the high school in the town that later became

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:47.959
the setting for portions of the book “11/22/63.”

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:52.319
Lisbon Falls was a milltown, and King spent
time working in the town’s mill when he

00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:54.650
was a teenager.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:04:00.660
College &amp; Career Start

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:06.590
After graduation, King had aspirations to
be a writer.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.720
So he headed north to the University of Maine
Orono to earn a degree in English Literature.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:15.330
While in college, King was a columnist for
the college paper, was outspoken against the

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.180
Vietnam War, and worked in the college library.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.269
That same library is now home to many of King’s
papers.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:23.059
It’s also where he met his wife.

00:04:23.059 --> 00:04:27.460
Tabitha was looking for a book in the stacks,
King passed by and struck up a conversation

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:32.499
with her, and four years later the literary
couple had graduated, had a daughter and was

00:04:32.499 --> 00:04:33.499
married.

00:04:33.499 --> 00:04:36.660
Though King had been writing in college, he
was far from being able to support himself

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.689
and a growing family just by writing.

00:04:38.689 --> 00:04:42.860
He, Tabitha, and their daughter Naomi were
living in a trailer outside of Bangor, and

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.499
King was working two jobs.

00:04:44.499 --> 00:04:48.550
He was teaching English at Hampden Academy,
and in the summers was pumping gas at the

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.589
local station while also working shifts at
a laundromat.

00:04:51.589 --> 00:04:55.490
Tabitha took on shifts at a Dunkin’ Donuts,
and they struggled to get by.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:58.009
But King always made time to write.

00:04:58.009 --> 00:05:02.119
Even in the cramped quarters of the family’s
trailer, he made a point to set aside space

00:05:02.119 --> 00:05:03.879
for a writing desk and typewriter.

00:05:03.879 --> 00:05:08.789
2,000 words a day was his goal, and that’s
a goal he stands by today.

00:05:08.789 --> 00:05:12.550
Eventually, King earned a teaching certificate
and was able to put his college education

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.090
to use as a teacher at Hampden Academy.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:18.199
The work still wasn’t what he wanted to
be doing, though.

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:22.960
It was writing he loved, and writing that
he wanted to earn a living from.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.789
Writing has always been his purpose in life,
as he explains, “There was nothing else

00:05:26.789 --> 00:05:27.789
I was made to do.

00:05:27.789 --> 00:05:30.639
I was made to write stories and I love to
write stories.

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:31.639
That's why I do it.

00:05:31.639 --> 00:05:35.830
I really can't imagine doing anything else
and I can't imagine not doing what I do."

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:39.539
Though it took years of effort, his big break
did come.

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:44.129
After Tabitha fished the beginning of Carrie
out of the trash in 1973, King finished the

00:05:44.129 --> 00:05:46.030
book and sent it off to a publisher.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:49.530
He wasn’t confident about its chances of
being published:

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:54.599
“...my considered opinion was that I had
written the world's all-time loser."

00:05:54.599 --> 00:05:58.470
The family wasn’t doing well financially
when he sent Carrie in, and they couldn’t

00:05:58.470 --> 00:05:59.470
even afford a phone.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:05.259
So he got the news via telegram … he’d
be receiving a 2500 dollar advance and Doubleday

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:06.930
would be publishing Carrie.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.659
Then...even better news.

00:06:08.659 --> 00:06:13.029
The paperback rights were sold for 400,000
dollars.

00:06:13.029 --> 00:06:17.949
King could quit teaching and write full-time,
and his family would be better off than they

00:06:17.949 --> 00:06:20.029
had ever dared to imagine.

00:06:20.029 --> 00:06:22.270
The book was a smash hit.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:26.419
Within the next year, a million copies were
sold and only three years later it was made

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.629
into an Academy Award winning movie.

00:06:28.629 --> 00:06:33.029
Stephen King was now officially a writer,
and an American celebrity.

00:06:33.029 --> 00:06:37.779
But success didn’t mean that King settled
into a completely untroubled life.

00:06:37.779 --> 00:06:41.930
As he kept writing, cranking out books like
Misery, Cujo, and Pet Sematary, he was drinking

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:44.289
heavily...and he knew he had a problem.

00:06:44.289 --> 00:06:45.860
King told Rolling Stone:

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.819
“Nobody in the house drank but me.

00:06:47.819 --> 00:06:50.300
My wife would have a glass of wine and that
was all.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.629
So I went in the garage one night, and the
trash can that was set aside for beer cans

00:06:53.629 --> 00:06:54.770
was full to the top.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:56.740
It had been empty the week before.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.659
I was drinking, like, a case of beer a night.

00:06:58.659 --> 00:07:00.740
And I thought, "I'm an alcoholic."

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:02.819
That was probably about '78, '79.

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:06.379
I thought, "I've gotta be really careful,
because if somebody says, ‘You're drinking

00:07:06.379 --> 00:07:09.229
too much, you have to quit,' I won't be able
to."

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:11.349
He knew he had a problem, but he didn’t
stop drinking.

00:07:11.349 --> 00:07:13.199
In fact, he took it one step further.

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:17.879
In the late 70s, King started doing cocaine,
using it at night while he was writing.

00:07:17.879 --> 00:07:22.059
By this time, he and Tabitha had three kids
and he knew his addictions were taking a toll

00:07:22.059 --> 00:07:24.360
both on his family and his writing.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.259
He attributes the length of Tommyknockers
to cocaine, and looking back has said the

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:32.119
book could have been half the length if so
much of it hadn’t been inspired purely by

00:07:32.119 --> 00:07:33.419
his drugged-up energy.

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:37.789
He’s also said he doesn’t even remember
writing Cujo, so bad was his consumption of

00:07:37.789 --> 00:07:39.800
alcohol and drugs during the 80s.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.139
"There's one novel, Cujo, that I barely remember
writing at all.

00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:47.860
I don't say that with pride or shame, only
with a vague sense of sorrow and loss.

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:48.889
I like that book.

00:07:48.889 --> 00:07:52.349
I wish I could remember enjoying the good
parts as I put them down on the page."

00:07:52.349 --> 00:07:56.779
And, The Shining and Misery both carry an
undercurrent of reference to his struggles

00:07:56.779 --> 00:08:01.460
- Jack Torrence is an alcoholic in The Shining,
and King has described the antagonist of Misery

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:04.560
as essentially the personification of cocaine.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.499
It wasn’t until the late 80s, when Tabitha
threatened to leave him and the family staged

00:08:08.499 --> 00:08:11.440
a dramatic intervention that King cleaned
up his act.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:15.669
At the intervention, his family displayed
drug paraphernalia and pills they had collected

00:08:15.669 --> 00:08:16.669
from the trash.

00:08:16.669 --> 00:08:20.539
King, with the magnitude of his problem laid
out in front of his eyes and in front of family

00:08:20.539 --> 00:08:23.020
and friends, made the decision to get sober.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:26.360
Now, he’s been sober for nearly three decades.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:30.939
During the 1990s and into the 21st century,
King continued to be one of the world’s

00:08:30.939 --> 00:08:32.980
most prolific and well-known writers.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:37.620
His books spawned movies and mini-series,
and over 350 million copies of his books have

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:38.720
been sold.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:42.190
After he became so popular, King took the
step of publishing under a pseudonym - Richard

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:43.190
Bachman.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:47.040
He wanted to see if he could still get books
published and have them sell without his now-famous

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.170
name splashed across the front.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.310
Turns out, he could.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.850
The first book he published under the name
Richard Bachman was “Rage.”

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:57.880
Set in King’s familiar world of a Maine
high school, the book tells the story of a

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:00.920
teenager who engages in violent acts at his
school.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:05.040
He sets his locker on fire, shoots his algebra
teacher, and attacks another student with

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.040
a wrench.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:08.580
In 1977, it was a figment of King’s imagination.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:14.570
But in the late 1980s and 1990s, the book
unfortunately started to resemble actual occurrences

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:17.640
at schools across the United States.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:21.960
In 1997, after a student shot eight people
at a prayer meeting in Kentucky, it was discovered

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:23.960
that he had a copy of Rage in his locker.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:27.390
Disturbed by this, King asked his publisher
to take the book out of print.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:37.570
To this day, Rage remains out of print.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:40.430
Accident

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.840
Even as his success grew, King remained living
in Maine.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.550
The family owns a home in Bangor, and a home
in Lovell near the lakes for the summer.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:52.690
It was near the home in Lovell that King’s
life almost ended in 1999.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:56.740
King was taking a walk along one of the winding,
wooded roads that are so familiar a part of

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:57.740
Maine’s landscape.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.190
Then, a van smashed into him from behind.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.750
King was knocked off the road, into a ditch.

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:06.240
Witnesses said he was in a heap and it was
clear his leg was broken.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.110
His glasses flew off, and landed in the van
that hit him.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:12.980
At the hospital, he underwent multiple surgeries
and had to do physical therapy as part of

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:14.050
his recovery.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:18.610
Bryan Smith, the driver who hit King, had
a track record of driving infractions, including

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:19.610
an OUI.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:23.420
The King accident was blamed on the Smith’s
dog distracting him, causing him to swerve

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:24.460
into the author.

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:29.080
Smith received a six month jail sentence that
was later suspended, and has his license revoked

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:30.080
for a year.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:35.270
King was upset … he wanted Smith’s license
revoked for life given his history of bad

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:36.270
driving.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:40.190
Only a year after the accident, Smith was
found dead in his trailer at the age of 43.

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:43.930
He died of a painkiller overdose, and, in
a twist of fate that seemed straight out of

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:48.150
a King novel - he died on King’s birthday,
September 21st.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:52.510
King sustained horrific injuries in the accident,
but he resumed writing only a month after

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:53.870
being released from the hospital.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:58.800
He finished the highly regarded “On Writing,”
and in 2000 the book was published, but by

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:03.160
2002 King decided that he simply didn’t
have the strength to keep writing as he had

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:04.160
in the past.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:08.080
Where he had previously sat and typed for
hours at a time, it now hurt him to sit for

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:09.490
long periods.

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:12.250
Addiction had also become part of his life
again.

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:16.350
After the accident, he took OxyContin to deal
with the pain from his injuries and became

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:17.430
addicted.

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:22.320
As he had a decade earlier, he was able to
overcome the addiction and live soberly.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:27.570
The lure of the written word ultimately proved
too strong for his post-accident injuries

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:30.430
and struggles, and so King returned to his
craft.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:34.510
He has said that he literally needs to write
to live.

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:35.710
What would happen if he didn’t write?

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:36.970
“'Oh, I'd be dead.

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:41.120
I would have drunk myself to death or drugged
myself to death or committed suicide or some

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:42.120
goddamn thing.''

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:46.900
Since his accident, he’s published dozens
of stories and books, including Mr. Mercedes,

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:50.180
Duma Key, 11/22/63, and Under The Dome.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:54.550
A diehard Red Sox fan, like so many of his
New England neighbors, Stephen King also co-wrote

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:58.160
a book after the Red Sox 2004 World Series
win.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:03.040
Faithful: Two Diehard Boston Red Sox Fans
Chronicle the Historic 2004 Season, shared

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:07.800
the story of the emotional roller coaster
King and so many other Red Sox fans rode throughout

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:09.320
the summer and fall of 2004.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:14.630
In a further display of his eclectic interests
and abillities, King dabbles in music.

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:18.980
He played guitar for “The Rock Bottom Remainders,”
a band whose other members you might recognize

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:23.230
too: Amy Tan, Dave Barry, and Matt Groening
are only a few of the other celebrities who

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:24.440
joined King on stage.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:27.580
And, he even co-wrote a musical with John
Mellencamp.

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:31.580
Billed as a “southern gothic supernatural
musical,” Ghost Brothers of Darkland County

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:35.360
opened ran for a month at the Alliance Theater
in Atlanta.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:41.120
King is 70 now, but hasn’t run out of ideas...in
fact, he and his son Owen just released a

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:45.530
co-written book in 2017.

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:53.370
Charitable Work &amp; Political Involvement

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:57.740
King is incredibly wealthy - he writes and
sells books at a breathtaking pace.

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:00.730
But he doesn’t use his wealth to amass ‘stuff.’

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:03.720
He and Tabitha own three houses - two in Maine
and one in Florida.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:09.470
It’s the house in Maine that is most iconic...located
in Bangor, it’s a huge old Victorian surrounded

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:12.560
by a wrought iron fence decorated with bats.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:16.670
From the outside, the house is exactly where
one would expect Stephen King to live.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:20.680
On the inside, it has an indoor swimming pool
and a huge underground library.

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:25.310
The houses are extravagant by everyday standards,
but they are really the primary way King prefers

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.330
to spend money on himself and Tabitha.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:28.600
“I'm not a clothes person.

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:29.600
I'm not a boat person.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:31.010
We do have a house in Florida.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:32.860
But we live in Maine, for Christ's sake.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:35.020
It's not like a trendy community or anything.”

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:38.830
His houses are beautiful, but they don’t
use all of his money … so what does he do

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:39.850
with it all?

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:41.230
Well, he gives it away, mostly.

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:44.910
Or, he invests in projects that support his
interests and his community.

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:48.120
A music fan, King has purchased radio stations
in Maine.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:51.950
A huge baseball fan, he funded the construction
of a Little League Field in Bangor.

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:56.410
The field is now known as “Field of Screams,”
a tip of the hat to the field’s funder.

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:59.920
Together, he and Tabitha run the Stephen and
Tabitha King Foundation.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:03.840
Libraries and colleges, especially the University
of Maine and the Bangor Public Library have

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.100
benefitted from the Kings’ philanthropy.

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:10.560
Maine’s historical societies, fire departments,
arts organizations, and hospitals have also

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:12.300
been beneficiaries of the Kings.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:17.980
In a 2001 speech at Vassar College, King made
his view on generosity and charity clear to

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:18.980
his audience.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:20.120
“Should you give away what you have?

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:21.150
Of course you should.

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:25.520
I want you to consider making your lives one
long gift to others, and why not?

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.280
All you have is on loan, anyway.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:30.790
All you want to get at the getting place,
from the Maserati you may dream about to the

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:34.750
retirement fund some broker will try to sell
you on, none of that is real.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:36.920
All that lasts is what you pass on.

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:38.810
The rest is smoke and mirrors.”

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:43.550
In that same speech, he discussed natural
resources and his dislike of the George W.

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:44.980
Bush administration.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:48.690
With the advent of Twitter in the years to
follow, King had an even wider audience to

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:50.100
share his political views with.

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:53.210
He’s take on President Trump via Twitter,
announcing that the President wasn’t allowed

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:56.930
to see the new version of “It” when it
hit theaters in 2017.

00:14:56.930 --> 00:15:01.630
He’s also made clear in very profane language...we
won’t repeat it here... what he thinks of

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.240
the President and his administration.

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:07.090
Stephen King is a national figure in his own
right.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:10.860
For thirty years he has been affecting the
conversation around pop culture.

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:15.840
His books sell by the millions, and his movies
rake in millions of dollars and win Academy

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:16.850
Awards.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:20.780
Growing up, he knew he wanted to write, but
he never wanted to do it to achieve the wealth

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:22.529
and fame he’s now amassed.

00:15:22.529 --> 00:15:26.240
His reasoning was much simpler, and it remains
his mantra to this day.

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:30.550
“Writing isn’t about making money, getting
famous, getting dates, getting laid, or making

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:31.550
friends.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:35.790
In the end, it’s about enriching the lives
of those who will read your work, and enriching

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:37.770
your own life, as well.”

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:41.610
For over three decades, from his home tucked
away in the northeast corner of the United

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:46.560
States, Stephen King has been able to live
out his childhood dream to write for a living.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:50.100
In doing so, he’s brought many of us nightmares
- but they’re nightmares the world gladly

00:15:50.100 --> 00:16:20.120
welcome as we continue to delve into his stories.

